PUBLIC RESPONSE: {26 and 74}

The poem is a double sestina whose form is defined by a fixed rotation of six terminal words (mountains/valleys/forests/music/morning/evening) that produces a rigid 12-stanza sequence plus a three-line envoi ending on valleys/music/evening.  

Every stanza in the given text obeys that rotation except at two positions: line 26 ends on “morning” and line 74 ends on “music.” Swapping those two lines restores the canonical terminal-word order at both locations while leaving the grammatical and rhetorical sense of the surrounding stanzas intact. All other lines already sit in their expected places, confirming that the only transposition required to recover the original form is 26 with 74.

