When the terminal words of every stanza are extracted and compared against that sequence, every position matches except two: line 28 ends “forests” where the rotation requires a later “forests” line, and line 35 ends “forests” where the earlier slot belongs. Both lines also open with the identical phrase “Long since my thoughts,” indicating a simple transposition. Swapping them restores the exact terminal-word order demanded by the sestina algorithm while leaving every other stanza untouched. No other pair produces the same clean restoration.

